Create a task on the board. The description is the spec — write it as a full markdown document a worker can execute with no conversation context. Include requirements, constraints, file paths, examples, and acceptance criteria. Always pre-fill subtasks as a checklist execution plan. Short title, rich description, concrete subtasks. A task's status advances across the board: `pending_approval` → `backlog` → `ready` → `in_progress` → `done`. New tasks start in `pending_approval`; ready tasks are picked up automatically by the cortex and executed by workers. Use `metadata` for structured external references like GitHub issues or PRs when the user mentions them, for example `{ "github_issue": { "repo": "owner/repo", "number": 123, "url": "https://github.com/owner/repo/issues/123" } }`.

Alongside the spec, a task carries its execution plan — where and how the work runs. Set `worker_type` (`opencode` for coding work, `builtin` otherwise), `project` (name or ID), and `worktree_mode`: `create` gives the task its own fresh worktree (the safe default for coding tasks — never runs in a dirty checkout), `existing` targets the worktree in `worktree_id`, `root` runs in the checkout itself (reserved for read-only work). Set `required_skills` for procedures the worker must follow — their full content is injected into the worker, unlike `suggested_skills` on spawn which are advisory. Fields you omit inherit the project's defaults. A task whose plan is complete can be approved and executed without another decision about where the work happens.

When work forms a pipeline, record the ordering with `depends_on` instead of describing it in prose — a dependent task stays blocked until its edges clear, so the board itself holds the plan. `gate` (default) waits for the dependency to finish; `stack` means this task builds directly on the dependency's branch and can start as soon as that branch exists.
